Z||000005||86SE||08-18-2000||03:12 AM||||I think I finally found it!  The rest of the info I need for a possible 3.4 pushrod Camaro swap!<P>OK.  So far I know that you have to relocate the starter to the other side of the engine, no problem.  And I now know that I have to change the front engine mount bracket, what all needs to be done to it?  Will it be pretty obvious when I goto put the engine on the cradle?<P>I have an auto tranny.  I know for sure that the 4 and 5 speeds bolt directly up the the engine.  What is different that the auto cannot bolt right onto the engine? (93-95 Camaro HT engine)<P>And my LAST question, I currently have an 86 V6, do I have to buy a new flywheel for the 93-95 Camaro engine for it to work on the Fiero?<P>Thats all!<P>Thanks for any replys!<P>------------------<BR>'86 Special Edition,<BR>High-Output V-6 Yellow Fiero<BR>Stering wheel<BR>Gas AND brake petals<BR>**Special Rally Heavy Duty Suspention**||||reg||10
Z||000006||mrfiero||08-18-2000||03:30 AM||||The use of the Camaro/Firebird 3.4 requires a neutrally balanced flywheel.  The '88 Fiero was the only one to use one....the other years were externally balanced.  That means you must buy an '88 Fiero flywheel or flexplate to do the swap.  I have heard that the 3.4 TDC flywheel is also neutrally balanced and is cheaper than the '88 Fiero flywheel (although it weighs a few pounds more).<P>The auto, tranny will bolt right up to the 3.4 without any problems.<P>||||reg||6
Z||000007||86SE||08-18-2000||03:38 AM||||Thanks mrfiero!  There is light at the end of the tunnel now!<P>About how much does a 3.4 TDC flywheel/flexplate cost?<P>Good to know my trans will fit!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>'86 Special Edition,<BR>High-Output V-6 Yellow Fiero<BR>Stering wheel<BR>Gas AND brake petals<BR>**Special Rally Heavy Duty Suspention**||||reg||10
Z||000008||mrfiero||08-18-2000||03:42 AM||||No problem 86SE.....I have no idea on their cost, but since you need a flexplate and not a flywheel (BIG difference) it  shouldn't be too much.  Let me sift through my parts....I might have a good used '88 Fiero flexplate for you.<BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||86SE||08-18-2000||03:54 AM||||Thanks again!!<P>You have been an even BIGGER help!  I just might get this swap done this winter, with not too many problems, hopefully!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>I found engines for about $500 + $200 shipping from Florida. from 40-65k miles.  I should have all the cash I need and then some by October.<P>------------------<BR>'86 Special Edition,<BR>High-Output V-6 Yellow Fiero<BR>Stering wheel<BR>Gas AND brake petals<BR>**Special Rally Heavy Duty Suspention**||||reg||10
Z||000010||ThatRickGuy||08-18-2000||11:32 AM||||to get the 3.4 to bolt up to the fiero auto tranny you need the '88 flexplate, starter swap and you need to grind off a little bit of the block to get it to bolt up correctly to the tranny. there's a few other little things also, from what I saw you could get a moderate mileage 3.4 and install it w/ all the headaches for arround $1000. or you could take your 2.8, stroke and bore it to a 3.2 and throw it back in for just a hair more. advantages of a professionally rebuild engine: No extra head ache to put back in, no extra grinding, 0 mileage, and Warentee!!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> mine is coming to $1500, but I'm getting everything balanced, head job, and tons of other work done at the same time. plus they're putting it together.||||reg||1
